FORMAT 1
SCENARIO Conflict
LABEL    Bonus
SUMMARY  Two branches changed the same line of the README. Git does not know which one is right – you decide.
INTRO    The same line in the README changed on the branch **main** and on the branch **edit**. Merging them causes a **conflict**.

SETUP
  PERSON jana NAME "Jana Nováková" EMAIL "novakovaj@jirovcovka.net" LOGIN "jana-novakova" LABEL "Jana's computer"
  MACHINE jana CONFIGURED
  REPOSITORY git/cookbook
  PS> mkdir git
  PS> cd git
  PS> mkdir cookbook
  PS> cd cookbook
  PS> git init
  FILE README.md
    # My cookbook

    Recipes I can cook.
  END
  FILE pancakes.md
    # Pancakes

    ## Ingredients

    - 250 ml milk
    - 1 egg
    - 120 g plain flour
    - a pinch of salt

    ## Tip

    I fill them with strawberry jam.
  END
  PS> git add .
  PS> git commit -m "Start the cookbook"
  PS> git switch -c edit
  FILE README.md
    # My cookbook

    Recipes that even I can cook.
  END
  PS> git commit -am "Edit description"
  PS> git switch main
  FILE README.md
    # My cookbook

    My favourite recipes.
  END
  PS> git commit -am "Different description"
  VSCODE git/cookbook
END

TASK Merge the branch **edit** into **main**.
  HINT     git merge edit
  QUESTION What did Git print? Which files have a conflict (git status)?
  CHECK    output of "git merge..." contains "CONFLICT"
END

TASK Open **README.md**. Between `<<<<<<<` and `=======` is your version, below it the other one. Keep what you want and **delete all the markers**. Save.
  NOTE  For example:
  HINT  # My cookbook
  HINT
  HINT  My favourite recipes.
  HINT  Recipes that even I can cook.
  CHECK file README.md has no conflict markers
  CHECK output of "git merge..." contains "CONFLICT"
END

TASK Mark the conflict as resolved and finish the merge with a commit.
  HINT  git add README.md
  HINT  git commit -m "Resolve conflict in README"
  HINT  git log --oneline --graph
  CHECK no merge is in progress
  CHECK HEAD is a merge
  CHECK file README.md in HEAD has no conflict markers
END
